SYNTHESIS
Our conversation about trust in institutions led us to cite religion and pop culture as two institutions, that are trusted and internalized by many Americans. I see these two as social constructs, that are utilized in a political and economic realm to push broader ideologies of hierarchy. I thought of a quote later to describe the influence of pop culture: as Guy Debord said in the Society of the Spectacle, the spectacle that mirrors reality must then infiltrate and amplify said reality. 

In Breakneck, Wang argues that the United States has a cultural emphasis on litigation and procedural law (there is an iconic scene in Fresh Off the Boat, in which after threatening to sue the school, the father does claim it is the American way). The US has developed extensive legal and regulatory protections, which do serve important purposes, such as protecting the minority, but these same systems can make it harder to quickly build infrastructure by imposing lengthy reviews and other precautionary measures on projects.

This, then, presents a tradeoff: a more rapid development and expansion in infrastructure can increase economic productivity and human capital, but legal and procedural safeguards can protect people from the costs of unchecked development. 

NEPA makes this tradeoff especially clear, as Potter argues that environmental review has become slow and expensive partly because agencies are concerned about litigation and therefore try to litigation-proof their project information. This makes one skeptical that simply streamlining NEPA would dramatically accelerate construction, as agencies would still have incentives to conduct extensive reviews if they were under the impression that insufficient documentation could expose them to lawsuits. 

Of course, eliminating environmental review entirely would create its own problems. Environmental regulations exist partly because infrastructure can impose costs on communities and ecosystems that are not reflected in the project’s private benefits. 

The challenge, therefore, is finding a system that allows the United States to build infrastructure while still identifying meaningful environmental consequences. Overall, I agree that America’s infrastructure is fundamentally an institutional-design problem. Streamlining individual regulations may help, but lasting change would require making the entire process more predictable and efficient while preserving the environmental and legal protections that give these institutions their legitimacy. 
